 Top-level `resourceStreamObservation` contains `observations`, additive `errors`, `finalized`, `collectionComplete`, `commandSuccess`, `completeResourceBody` and `accepted`. A record contains the typed command/reply, CDP-only candidates/events/terminals, count-only rows, piece hashes/lengths, bounds, handoff status, final request admission and body/artifact/publication fields. `collectionComplete` requires a settled attempted command, one known terminal, final request admission, normal browser close and durable publication without uncertain collection errors. A recorded command rejection may be fully collected and INCONCLUSIVE: commandSuccess/completeResourceBody/accepted remain false. Unresolved commands, overflow, lost payload, bad framing/metadata, timeout, failed IO or source checks cannot satisfy collectionComplete.
 
-`completeResourceBody` additionally requires a successful handoff, consistent retained payloads, positive actual data and one loadingFinished. loadingFailed can retain an observed prefix but never yields a complete body. `resource-stream-observed.bin` contains the buffered prefix followed by observed suffix pieces; its assembly/contiguousFromStart/complete fields explicitly distinguish a qualified prefix, incomplete pieces and a complete resource. `resource-stream-request.bin` retains the admitted fresh form bytes. Both files use exclusive creation, fsync, close and byte/hash readback before the final browser result is saved. Earlier inherited progress saves remain progress records. Normal browser closure precedes final observer publication. No extra network wait is added to the original lifecycle.
+`completeResourceBody` additionally requires a successful handoff, consistent retained payloads (including a legitimate zero-byte resource) and one loadingFinished. loadingFailed can retain an observed prefix but never yields a complete body. `resource-stream-observed.bin` contains the buffered prefix followed by observed suffix pieces; its assembly/contiguousFromStart/complete fields explicitly distinguish a qualified prefix, incomplete pieces and a complete resource. `resource-stream-request.bin` retains the admitted fresh form bytes. Both files use exclusive creation, fsync, close and byte/hash readback before the final browser result is saved. Earlier inherited progress saves remain progress records. Normal browser closure precedes final observer publication. No extra network wait is added to the original lifecycle.
 
 The original strict `passed` remains separate. Root's new full-client/runtime protocol must evaluate the additive collection fields explicitly for comparison continuation and must retain all strict failures. Complete CDP resource bytes do not establish React root settlement, `reader.read().done`, stream cancellation initiator, MPA navigation, HTTP wire framing, TCP/host completion, private Store identity or cause. The observer adds browser/DevTools buffering, IPC, allocation and file IO, so a changed outcome alone is not a reliability, timing or GC explanation.
 
